WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.260
- I am so incredibly
honored to be here tonight

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:07.508
to celebrate the great Carol Littleton.

00:00:07.508 --> 00:00:10.556
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:00:10.556 --> 00:00:13.140
Where are you, there you are.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:17.820
I think it's safe to say
that all of us are here,

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:22.140
because we have an abiding
passion to create stories

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:25.510
that will resonate in
the human heart and soul

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.480
and will help us understand who we are,

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.030
where we came from, where we're going,

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:36.450
and why we are feeling what we feel.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:37.860
And I think all of us would agree

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:42.150
that films are ultimately
created in the editing room.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:47.150
After the pressure, passion,
and drama of shooting a movie,

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.470
after the actors and the production crews

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:53.430
have all dispersed,
hopefully to other jobs,

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:56.010
it is in the editing
room that the director

00:00:56.010 --> 00:01:00.420
and editor take all
the chosen raw material

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.443
and attempt to fashion it
into something extraordinary.

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:09.420
I'm convinced that one of
the most important elements

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:13.260
in all good movies and
an absolute imperative

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:17.550
in all great movies is
the emotional connection

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:21.180
that's established with the
audience in the first act

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:26.180
and is maintained and enhanced
up to the final moment.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:29.460
The delicacy of that connection

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:31.623
is the mark of a great editor.

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:37.260
Great editing makes us
care, thus rearranging us

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:41.973
in a particular moment or
sometimes for a lifetime.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:46.290
A great editor is an advocate,

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:51.290
a detective, a humanist, a philosopher,

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:55.383
and in some cases a sensitive
and articulate teacher.

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:58.890
They see things that a
director could have forgotten

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:02.760
or doesn't even know he or she even has,

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:07.020
a turn of a head, the blink of an eye,

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:10.800
the emotional beat that's been truncated

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:12.660
or an elusive moment without which

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:16.380
the connection to the audience
would be irrevocably lost.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.513
It is that delicate.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:24.440
We are a complex and conflicted species,

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:30.020
and so are our stories,
something fully understood

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:32.940
by skillful editors.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:37.680
Carol Littleton is a great humanist.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.850
She loves us with all
our faults and foibles.

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:46.850
She does not judge us. She's
after the truth of a moment.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.950
She knows that in our ignorance,

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:53.916
despite ourselves, if led gently,

00:02:53.916 --> 00:02:57.420
or not so gently by the hand,

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:01.533
we have the collective
capacity to sense what is true,

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:07.520
and it is that truth that
moves us, that changes us.

00:03:08.805 --> 00:03:12.330
"The Big Chill" was my second film.

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:14.460
Thank you, Larry, for trusting

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:18.180
that the strange and
eccentric nurse, Jenny Fields,

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:20.378
in "The World According to Garp,"

00:03:20.378 --> 00:03:21.211
(audience clapping and cheers)

00:03:21.211 --> 00:03:25.740
could also be this nurturing
steady, I thought for a while,

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:30.090
boring Sarah Cooper, in "The Big Chill."

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:34.470
We became a family with
two sets of parents,

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:39.393
Larry and Meg Kasdan and John
Bailey and Carol Littleton.

00:03:40.991 --> 00:03:43.991
(audience clapping)

00:03:47.554 --> 00:03:51.780
And though time and
distance has separated us

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:54.573
through the years, we remain a family.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:58.380
Indeed, we have endured our first losses,

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:02.910
the great loved,
humanly-conflicted, Bill Hurt,

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:07.470
and our father, mentor,
friend, and industry leader,

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:10.925
John Bailey, Carol's beloved husband.

00:04:10.925 --> 00:04:13.925
(audience clapping)

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:19.680
For me, "The Big Chill"
was the true beginning

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.773
of my education in the
craft of film acting.

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:28.610
I was so incredibly lucky to
be in such brilliant hands,

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:33.270
because in retrospect, it
was really the wordless,

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:37.890
beautifully edited moments
that gave Sarah Cooper

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:40.950
her emotional weight with the audience,

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:42.900
crying in the shower,

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:46.050
joining the group after
crying in the shower,

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:49.443
smiling at Mary Kay after
she'd had sex with my husband,

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:52.600
if you haven't seen the movie, see it,

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:56.163
and dancing in the kitchen.

00:04:57.807 --> 00:05:01.890
I really thank you, Carol, for
putting my butt in that shot,

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:04.770
because it made me look
like a really good dancer,

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:08.459
even though I was by far the worst.

00:05:08.459 --> 00:05:10.440
It was while filming "The Big Chill"

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:14.133
that I learned the power
of thought in film.

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:20.270
But you know, you can act
until your panties fray

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:25.800
and the cows come home, and
it won't resonate for a second

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:29.010
unless you were led by a John Bailey,

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:30.880
directed by a Larry Kasdan

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:36.860
and edited with love,
intelligence, empathy, and rigor

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:43.620
by none other than the
truly great human being,

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:46.993
Carol Littleton.
(bright music)

